    "title": "South Sudan President Dissolves Transitional Government Ahead of Elections",
    "original_title": "Madaxweynaha Koonfurta Suudaan oo kala diray xukuumaddii ku-meelgaarka ahayd",
    "slug": "south-sudan-president-dissolves-transitional-government-ahead-of-elections",
    "summary": "President Salva Kiir Mayardit has dissolved South Sudan's transitional unity government and appointed an interim administration, removing the vice presidents, ministers, and some local officials.",
    "content": "South Sudan's President Salva Kiir Mayardit issued a decree on Wednesday dissolving the country's transitional unity government. The order removes the vice presidents, ministers, and a number of regional administrators from their positions. It also dissolves both national and state-level legislatures, while judicial and security institutions continue to operate.\n\nThe decree comes one day after South Sudan's parliament approved a measure extending the transitional period, according to the original report. The government had been formed under a 2018 peace agreement that ended a five-year civil war. That deal established a power-sharing arrangement between Kiir and opposition leader Riek Machar, who has served as first vice president.\n\nThe dissolution effectively ends the power-sharing government that was meant to lead the country to its first national elections. No date has been set for the polls, and it remains unclear how the new interim administration will function or how long it will serve. The move also leaves the status of the vice presidents and ministers uncertain.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the development is significant because South Sudan is a fellow member of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD), a regional bloc that has mediated in both South Sudanese and Somali conflicts. Many Somalis in the diaspora follow IGAD's political dynamics, as they often influence peace and security efforts in the Horn of Africa.\n\nAdditionally, South Sudan hosts a large Somali business community, particularly in Juba, where Somali traders are active in the retail and construction sectors. Political instability there can affect their operations and safety. The dissolution also sets a precedent for how transitional governments in the region handle delayed elections, which could have implications for Somalia's own political transition.\n\nPresident Kiir's decree marks a major shift in South Sudan's political landscape as the country prepares for elections. The international community has yet to respond to the dissolution. Observers will be watching to see whether the new interim administration includes opposition figures or consolidates power in the presidency.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/23/madaxweynaha-koonfurta-suudaan-oo-kala-diray-xukuumaddii-ku-meelgaarka-ahayd/",
