    "id": "news-1790101524612-2",
    "title": "Uganda Suspends Defense Cooperation with Turkey Amid Somalia Mission Tensions",
    "original_title": "Uganda and Turkey clash over military presence in Somalia",
    "slug": "uganda-suspends-defense-cooperation-with-turkey-amid-somalia-mission-tensions",
    "summary": "Uganda's military has suspended all defense cooperation with Turkey, a move linked to the potential end of the African Union mission in Somalia, which could cost Kampala tens of millions in lost revenue.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU – The Uganda People's Defence Forces (UPDF) announced on Saturday that it has suspended all defense and military cooperation with Turkey with immediate effect. The decision, reported by Ugandan media, followed demonstrations outside the Turkish embassy in Kampala by supporters of a particular group. Several sources familiar with the matter told Middle East Eye that the suspension is connected to the possible conclusion of the African Union mission in Somalia, which could result in Uganda losing tens of millions of dollars in revenue.\n\nThe African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) is set to end in December 2024, after which security responsibilities will be transferred to Somali forces. Uganda has been a major troop-contributing country to the mission, earning significant funds for its military. Turkey, meanwhile, has deepened its ties with Somalia in recent years, providing military training and equipment, and has a military base in Mogadishu. The two countries' interests in Somalia have increasingly overlapped, leading to friction.\n\nUganda's suspension of defense cooperation with Turkey is a significant development in the broader competition for influence in Somalia. Turkey has been expanding its presence in the Horn of Africa, while Uganda seeks to maintain its role as a key security provider. The loss of revenue from ATMIS would be a major blow to Uganda's military budget, which has relied on the mission's payments. The demonstrations in Kampala, reportedly by supporters of the Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), a Ugandan rebel group, add a domestic political dimension to the dispute.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development underscores the fragility of international support for Somalia's security. Many in the diaspora have family members affected by the ongoing conflict and rely on stability for remittances and travel. The potential end of ATMIS and the rift between Uganda and Turkey could complicate efforts to maintain security gains, potentially leading to a vacuum that armed groups might exploit. The diaspora has long called for sustained international engagement to prevent Somalia from sliding back into chaos.\n\nSeveral sources indicated that the suspension is directly linked to the planned withdrawal of ATMIS and the financial implications for Uganda. The UPDF's statement did not provide further details, and it remains unclear whether the suspension is permanent or temporary. Turkish officials have not publicly responded to the announcement. The incident highlights the complex web of alliances and economic interests that shape Somalia's security landscape.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/uganda-turkey-clash-military-presence-somalia/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/uganda-turkey-clash-military-presence-somalia/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Security",
    "published_at": "2026-09-22T14:11:16.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-09-22T18:25:24.612Z",
    "update_batch": "afternoon",
