    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo ku baaqday iskaashi caalami ah oo lagu xoojinayo badbaadada carruurta",
    "slug": "somali-minister-joins-global-call-for-child-safety-in-digital-systems",
    "summary": "At a UN General Assembly side event in New York, Somalia's Minister of Family and Human Rights Development highlighted the need for international cooperation to protect children online.",
    "content": "New York — On September 22, 2026, Ambassador Khadiija Maxamed Al-Makhzoumi, Minister of Family and Human Rights Development of the Federal Government of Somalia, took part in a high-level meeting in New York focused on child protection and the development of safe digital systems for children. The event, titled “The Invisible Architecture: Building Safe Digital Systems for Children – Advancing International Standards and Shared Responsibility,” was held during the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA81).\n\nThe meeting brought together representatives from various countries and international organizations to discuss strategies for safeguarding children in the digital age. It emphasized the need for global standards and shared responsibility among nations to ensure that digital platforms do not harm young users. Minister Al-Makhzoumi's participation underscores Somalia's commitment to engaging with international partners on child welfare issues.\n\nThis event follows ongoing efforts by the Somali government to strengthen child protection frameworks, both domestically and through international collaboration. In recent years, Somalia has been working to address challenges such as child marriage, recruitment of child soldiers, and limited access to education, while also navigating the rapid spread of digital technology. The minister's attendance at UNGA81 signals a recognition that digital safety is an emerging frontier in child protection.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly relevant. Many diaspora families raise children in countries with advanced digital infrastructures, and they face the universal challenge of keeping children safe online. The Somali government's active role in these global discussions may lead to policies and partnerships that benefit diaspora communities as well, by promoting best practices and resources that can be shared across borders.\n\nMinister Al-Makhzoumi's engagement at the UN also highlights the Somali government's broader efforts to project a positive image internationally and to seek cooperation on social development issues. As digital technology continues to shape the lives of children worldwide, coordinated action between governments, tech companies, and civil society will be essential to create safer online environments.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/22/soomaaliya-oo-ku-baaqday-iskaashi-caalami-ah-oo-lagu-xoojinayo-badbaadada-carruurta/",
